   "text": "Britain stayed out for over a decade. Then, on October 8th, 1990, it jumped in. The rate: one pound for 2.95 Deutschmarks. The band: six percent either way. The floor: 2.778. Below that, the Bank of England had to buy pounds, whatever it cost. The man who signed Britain up was the finance minister, Chancellor John Major. The same day, rates were cut from 15 to 14 percent."
